<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kathiawar Agency</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as British Kathiawar Agency</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Kathiawar</a> Agency was the British political agency, based at Rajkot, that supervised the more than two hundred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> and chiefships of the Kathiawar peninsula in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, from great states such as Junagadh and Bhavnagar to tiny talukas. It settled disputes and tribute owed to Baroda and the British. In 1924 it was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-india-states-agency">Western India States Agency</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kathiawar-agency.jpg" alt="Kathiawar Agency" width="320" height="288" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John George Bartholomew · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baroda_state_1909.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The ruler held a few villages and, after 1807, came under British supervision in the <b>Kathiawar Agency</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/amarnagar-state">Amarnagar State</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1807 Bajana came under British supervision in the <b>Kathiawar Agency</b> as a minor non-salute state.</blockquote><a href="/state/bajana-state">Bajana State</a></li><li><blockquote>Under rulers such as Takhtsinhji it built railways, colleges and hospitals and was regarded as one of the better-administered states of the <b>Kathiawar Agency</b>, with Prabhashankar Pattani as a noted diwan.</blockquote><a href="/state/bhavnagar-state">Bhavnagar State</a></li><li><blockquote>Little is recorded about Bilkha beyond its listing as a minor state of the <b>Kathiawar Agency</b> held by Kathi Darbars.</blockquote><a href="/state/bilkha-state">Bilkha State</a></li><li><blockquote>The Thakor ruled a territory of cotton and grain villages, paying tribute to the Gaekwads and then coming under British supervision in the <b>Kathiawar Agency</b> after 1807.</blockquote><a href="/state/chuda-state">Chuda State</a></li><li><blockquote>The state paid tribute to the Gaekwads of Baroda and, after 1807, came under British supervision in the <b>Kathiawar Agency</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/dasada-state">Dasada State</a></li></ul></section>
